program            = 007_struct_copy_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Aggregate construction, return by value, assignment copy and pass by value unchanged at -O0, -O1 and -O2
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow: the largest value computed
is sum_large at 249. Every member of every aggregate is assigned before it is read:
make_small sets both members, make_boundary all four, make_mixed both, and make_large
initializes checksum to zero, then writes all eight elements of v and sets flag, so no
member is ever read uninitialized. Array indices run from 0 to 7 on an eight-element array,
strictly in bounds. No padding byte is ever read, printed or compared: only named members
are accessed, no struct is compared with memcmp, and no object representation is inspected.
No shift, no division, no pointer arithmetic, no aliasing violation, and no struct is
accessed through a pointer of a different type. No object is modified twice between sequence
points, and the sum_small, sum_boundary and sum_large arguments are pure, so no printf
argument expression has a side effect. No dependence on relative addresses.
Several volatile objects are read within a single expression; their relative order is
unspecified, but no volatile object is modified anywhere in the program, so every read of a
given object yields the same value and the printed output does not depend on the ordering.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int and double
are used, both of fixed width on all four targets, and long is never used because sizeof(long)
is 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three targets. long double is deliberately avoided because
its representation was measured at 16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes across the four targets; that
hazard belongs to the floating-point area, not here. Plain char is not used, so the measured
plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V
64) cannot be observed. No address, pointer value, sizeof, _Alignof or offsetof result is
printed, and no padding byte is observed, so the differing aggregate layouts and alignment
rules of the four ABIs have no observable effect. The double value 6.75 is exactly
representable in binary floating point and is printed with %.3f, fixed precision with margin,
which was measured byte-identical across all twelve target and optimization configurations.
The aggregates deliberately straddle each target's by-register versus by-memory threshold:
small at 8 bytes, boundary at exactly 16 bytes, mixed at 16 bytes spanning two register
classes, and large at 40 bytes passed in memory everywhere. i686 passes every aggregate on
the stack while the other three targets use a 16-byte threshold, and how each compiler
implements an aggregate copy, whether by register moves, a memcpy equivalent or field by
field, is at its discretion. None of that is observable; only the printed member values are.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
small a=5 b=10
small_copy a=5 b=10
small_byvalue=15
boundary a=5 b=6 c=7 d=8
boundary_copy a=5 b=6 c=7 d=8
boundary_byvalue=26
mixed tag=5 value=6.750
mixed_copy tag=5 value=6.750
large v[0]=5 copy_v[0]=5
large v[1]=8 copy_v[1]=8
large v[2]=11 copy_v[2]=11
large v[3]=14 copy_v[3]=14
large v[4]=17 copy_v[4]=17
large v[5]=20 copy_v[5]=20
large v[6]=23 copy_v[6]=23
large v[7]=26 copy_v[7]=26
large checksum=124 flag=1
large_copy checksum=124 flag=1
large_byvalue=249
END
